  "question": "What has been proposed to reduce the risk of a learned optimizer tailoring its updates to guesses about future training batches, and by what mechanism?",
  "reference_answer": "Modify the meta-training loss of the RNN that generates damping parameters by adding l(f(xt,wt+1),yt): the loss on the current mini-batch evaluated after the parameter update. The RNN tends to predict and fit its next input, but future mini-batches are unpredictable, so this tendency can lead to poor fitting behavior and difficulty learning early in meta-training. The added term evaluates the update against the already observed batch as well as the next batch, reducing the influence of attempts to anticipate future inputs and thereby stabilizing meta-training. The per-step losses are combined using a softmax-weighted average.",
  "spans": [
    "RNN has the tendency to predict the next input and to fit for it, but the mini-batch xt is indeed unpredictable in meta-training",
    "which might cause overfitting or be hard to train at the early stage.",
    "with some modifications by adding the second item l(f(xt,wt+1),yt)",
    "can reduce such influence and so stabilize meta-training process. Thus, ls is the softmax weighted average over all lts."
  ]
}
